</section>

<section id="s-wall" data-code="wall">
<div class="section-no">02 &mdash; Data Wall</div>
<h2>Why EAGLE-2 Hit a Data Ceiling</h2>
<p>EAGLE-1 and EAGLE-2 autoregressively predict hidden <em>features</em> rather than tokens directly, adding a feature prediction loss l_fea on top of l_token. This acts as a structural constraint: at draft step 1, the model predicts f_hat(t+1). At step 2, the input is f_1,&hellip;,f_t, f_hat(t+1) &mdash; but f_hat(t+1) &ne; f(t+1). The draft falls off its training distribution immediately. Adding more data cannot fix a distribution shift that is architectural in origin.</p>
<div class="vp" id="vp-wall">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">data scaling law &mdash; EAGLE-2 saturates, EAGLE-3 keeps climbing</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click buttons to overlay</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="ds-tab-btn active" id="wall-e2" onclick="drawWall('eagle2')">EAGLE-2</button>
      <button class="ds-tab-btn" id="wall-e3" onclick="drawWall('eagle3')">EAGLE-3</button>
      <button class="ds-tab-btn" id="wall-both" onclick="drawWall('both')">Both (compare)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="wall-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.67rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem">X: data scale relative to ShareGPT (1&times; &asymp; 120K conversations). Y: acceptance length on MT-bench. EAGLE-2 plateaus past 2&times;; EAGLE-3 keeps climbing to 8&times;. [Source: EAGLE-3 Fig.1, arXiv:2503.01840]</div>
  </div>
</div>
<p>From EAGLE-3's Figure 1: at 1&times; ShareGPT, EAGLE-2 acceptance length &asymp; 3.70; at 8&times;, barely 3.75. EAGLE-3 goes from &asymp; 3.80 at 1&times; to &asymp; 4.40 at 8&times; &mdash; a 16% improvement from data alone, possible only because the architecture was fixed first.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-scale" data-code="scale">
<div class="section-no">03 &mdash; Scale Curve</div>
<h2>EAGLE-3's Architecture Unlocks Data Scaling</h2>
<p>EAGLE-3 makes two changes. First, it <strong>drops the feature prediction loss</strong> &mdash; the draft model predicts tokens directly with no l_fea constraint. Second, it introduces <strong>Training-Time Test (TTT)</strong>: during training, after the draft generates its first token a(t+1), the model is fed that prediction and trained to continue drafting &mdash; simulating the actual multi-step inference loop.</p>
<div class="vp" id="vp-ttt">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">training-time test &mdash; simulating inference-time error accumulation during training</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">navigate steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="ttt-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Prefix: standard training sequence</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The draft model receives multi-layer features from the target model (low, mid, high layers fused). It produces predicted token a(t+1). Feature prediction loss l_fea is removed entirely.</div>
          <div class="step-code">input: [f_low, f_mid, f_high, &hellip;, f_t]  &rarr;  predict: a(t+1)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">TTT step 1: feed back the draft token</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The draft prediction a(t+1) (not ground-truth f(t+1)) is appended to the context. The model attends to both the training prefix and its own previous draft output &mdash; matching inference behaviour exactly.</div>
          <div class="step-code">input: [f_1, &hellip;, f_t, a(t+1)]  &rarr;  predict: a(t+2)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">TTT step 2: continue drafting from imperfect state</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The chain continues, training the model to handle compounding error &mdash; the core distribution mismatch EAGLE-2 could never recover from. The model learns to draft robustly even when earlier predictions were wrong.</div>
          <div class="step-code">input: [f_1, &hellip;, f_t, a(t+1), a(t+2)]  &rarr;  predict: a(t+3)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Multi-layer feature fusion: richer input signal</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Instead of only top-layer features, EAGLE-3 fuses low-, mid-, and high-level features &mdash; richer semantic signal that benefits from more diverse training data. This is what enables the data scaling law.</div>
          <div class="step-code">features: FC([f_low, f_mid, f_high])  &rarr;  draft input</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="ttt-prev" disabled>&larr; Prev</button>
      <button id="ttt-next">Next &rarr;</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="ttt-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Key result: EAGLE-3 trained with &asymp;8&times; more data achieves a <strong>1.4&times; latency speedup over EAGLE-2</strong> at batch size 1. In SGLang at batch=64, EAGLE-3 achieves a 40% throughput improvement. [arXiv:2503.01840]</p>
</section>

<section id="s-compose" data-code="compose">
<div class="section-no">04 &mdash; Composition</div>
<h2>What Goes Into the Mix</h2>
<p>Once the architecture unlocks data scaling, the question becomes: which data? The three main production recipes differ substantially in size, domain balance, and source selection.</p>
<div class="vp" id="vp-compose">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">dataset composition &mdash; three recipes compared</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to switch recipe</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ds-tabs">
      <button class="ds-tab-btn active" onclick="showRecipe(0)">EAGLE-3 Baseline (320K)</button>
      <button class="ds-tab-btn" onclick="showRecipe(1)">SpecBundle / Perfect-Blend (1.4M)</button>
      <button class="ds-tab-btn" onclick="showRecipe(2)">DFlash (800K)</button>
    </div>
    <div class="ds-total">Total: <span id="ds-total-val">320K conversations</span></div>
    <div id="ds-bars"></div>
    <div style="font-size:.67rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem" id="ds-note">Original dataset responses. Limited math/code coverage.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<h3>EAGLE-3 Baseline: 320K Generic Conversations</h3>
<p>The released EAGLE-3 drafts were trained on <strong>ShareGPT (120K)</strong> and <strong>UltraChat-200K</strong>, totaling &asymp;320K conversations. Both are dialogue-heavy and math/code-light. The drafts work well for chat but under-serve math and coding tasks.</p>
<h3>SpecBundle: 1.4M Balanced with Target-Regenerated Responses</h3>
<p>SpecForge's SpecBundle uses <strong>Open-PerfectBlend</strong> (Xu et al., 2024) &mdash; 1.4M conversations balanced across chat, math, coding, and instruction-following. Responses re-generated by target model at temperature 0.8. Training: 2 epochs, lr=1e-4, cosine annealing. [SpecForge arXiv:2603.18567]</p>
<h3>DFlash: 800K Nemotron + CodeAlpaca</h3>
<p>DFlash uses &asymp;<strong>800K samples from NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training V2</strong> mixed with <strong>CodeAlpaca</strong>. All responses are target-model-generated.</p>
<table class="results-table" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Recipe</th><th>Size</th><th>Domains</th><th>Responses</th><th>&tau; (Math500)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>EAGLE-3 baseline</td><td class="mn">320K</td><td>Chat only</td><td>Original</td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--amber)">2.82</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row">
      <td>SpecBundle (Perfect-Blend)</td><td class="mn">1.4M</td><td>Chat+Math+Code+Instruct</td><td>Target-regen T=0.8</td><td class="mn best">3.48</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>DFlash (Nemotron+CodeAlpaca)</td><td class="mn">800K</td><td>Math+Code+Chat</td><td>Target-regen</td><td class="mn best">~6.49 avg &tau; *</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted)">* DFlash &tau; measured on Qwen3-8B with block size 16 &mdash; not directly comparable to EAGLE-3 on LLaMA. [Sources: SpecForge arXiv:2603.18567; DFlash]</p>
</section>

<section id="s-regen" data-code="regen">
<div class="section-no">05 &mdash; Regeneration</div>
<h2>The Target-Regeneration Trick</h2>
<p>Both SpecBundle and DFlash <strong>regenerate all dataset responses using the target model</strong> before training. A speculative draft model's job is to predict what <em>this specific target model</em> would say. Internet-scraped or GPT-generated responses use different phrasing, verbosity, and code styles than LLaMA-3.1-8B or Qwen3-8B.</p>
<div class="vp" id="vp-regen">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">response regeneration &mdash; closing the train/inference distribution gap</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">navigate steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="regen-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Original dataset response (e.g., from ChatGPT)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Prompt: "Solve 2x + 3 = 7." Original: "Sure! Let's solve step by step: 2x = 7 &minus; 3 = 4, so x = 2. Hope that helps! &#128522;" &mdash; verbose, emoji, hedged.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Style: verbose, emoji, first-person hedging</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target model's actual response (LLaMA-3.1-8B)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Same prompt &rarr; "2x + 3 = 7 &rarr; 2x = 4 &rarr; x = 2" &mdash; terse, no emoji, direct. A draft trained on the original response predicts the wrong style and gets rejected more often.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Style: concise, direct, no hedging</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Regenerate with target model at T=0.8</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run every prompt through the target model at temperature 0.8 and save the output as the new training label. T=0.8 (not greedy) introduces diversity across epochs, acting as implicit data augmentation.</div>
          <div class="step-code">response = target_model.generate(prompt, temperature=0.8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Result: near-zero distribution mismatch</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The draft is trained to predict exactly what the target would say. P-EAGLE found context-length mismatch alone causes 25% acceptance rate drops. Style mismatch is analogous. Both SpecForge and DFlash adopt this independently. [SpecForge &sect;6; DFlash &sect;4.2]</div>
          <div class="step-code">SpecForge: regeneration required for "best performance"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="regen-prev" disabled>&larr; Prev</button>
      <button id="regen-next">Next &rarr;</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="regen-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-dflash" data-code="dflash">
<div class="section-no">06 &mdash; DFlash Recipe</div>
<h2>DFlash's Divergent Training Recipe</h2>
<p>Block diffusion drafters require a fundamentally different data format. Where EAGLE-3 trains by teacher-forcing through the response sequence, DFlash trains a <em>denoising model</em> that predicts entire blocks of masked tokens in one parallel pass.</p>
<h3>800K Samples: Nemotron + CodeAlpaca</h3>
<p>DFlash collects &asymp;<strong>800K samples</strong> from NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training Dataset V2 mixed with CodeAlpaca. All responses are regenerated by the target model. DFlash's anchor-sampling strategy provides implicit data augmentation, extracting multiple training examples per response.</p>
<h3>Anchor Sampling: One Response, Many Training Blocks</h3>
<p>DFlash <strong>randomly samples anchor tokens</strong> from the response, each anchor becoming the first (clean) token of a draft block, with the next block_size &minus; 1 positions masked. This directly matches inference-time behaviour: each verification cycle always starts from a clean "bonus token." DFlash's ablation (Table 9) shows this "substantially improves both acceptance length and speedup."</p>
<div class="vp" id="vp-dflash">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">DFlash block training &mdash; anchor sampling + exponential loss weights</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:1rem;align-items:center">
      <button id="dflash-play" style="padding:.3rem .75rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);border-radius:5px;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);cursor:pointer">&#9654; Play</button>
      <button id="dflash-reset" style="padding:.3rem .75rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);border-radius:5px;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);cursor:pointer">&#8635; Reset</button>
      <span id="dflash-status" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">t = 0.0</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="dflash-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:rgba(26,26,46,0.6)"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.67rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem">Blue: prompt tokens. Yellow anchor (r1): clean starting token. Green&rarr;purple: masked tokens predicted in parallel. Right bar: loss weights w_k = exp(-(k-1)/16). [Source: DFlash &sect;4.2]</div>
  </div>
</div>
<h3>Loss Weighting: Prioritizing Early Positions</h3>
<p>Errors cascade in speculative decoding: if the draft gets position 0 wrong, all subsequent positions are rejected. DFlash applies an exponentially decaying weight to the cross-entropy loss:</p>
<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">DFlash positional loss weight [DFlash &sect;4.2, Eq.4]</div>
w_k = exp( -(k-1) / <span class="math-var">&gamma;</span> )   <span class="math-comment">k = position within block (1-indexed), &gamma; = block size = 16</span>

k=1:  w = 1.000   <span class="math-comment">&larr; first masked position, full weight</span>
k=4:  w &asymp; 0.832
k=8:  w &asymp; 0.646
k=15: w &asymp; 0.418   <span class="math-comment">&larr; last position, lower weight</span>
</div>
<h3>Shared Embedding and LM Head (Frozen)</h3>
<p>DFlash's draft model shares the frozen target model's token embedding layer and LM head. Only the 5 transformer layers of the draft are trained &mdash; reducing trainable parameters by 30&ndash;40% and forcing tight distributional alignment.</p>
</section>

